okay today I'm going to talk to you  about distillation we're going to look  at simple distillation we're going to  see a fractional distillation we're  going to see the equipment required and  how it works and the difference between  simple distillation or fractional  distillation okay so let's get started  now this here that we're looking at is  an example of simple distillation you  can use simple distillation when you've  got a mixture of two substances that  have very different boiling points so  let's just imagine here we have a salty  water so the reason why we use new  simple distillation is because the  difference in boiling point between  water and salt sodium chloride is  massive water has a boiling point of 100  degrees C whereas sodium chloride has a  boiling point of 1413 degree C so  because there's a huge difference in  boiling point we can use simple  distillation so what we have here at the  bottom is a Bunsen burner now this could  also be a heating mantle we just need to  heat up this salty water until we get to  the boiling point of water so when this  reaches a hundred degree C we will have  evaporation taking place  so remember evaporation means turning  from a liquid to a gas only the water  evaporates at this stage the salt does  not evaporate because we need a huge  huge amount of heat in order to  evaporate the sodium chloride so it's  only the water that evaporates here we  can see we have steam that starts to  travel down this part here now obviously  the whole point is we want to be able to  collect our liquid water at the end we  don't want steam so we need to turn the  steam back into liquid water and to do  that we use this bit of equipment here  which is called a condenser this thing  here now the condenser needs to cool  down the steam to turn it back from a  gas into a liquid now the way that that  works is we have cold water which flows  in this part here called the condenser  now if you look really really carefully  you'll see that there are kind of two  tubes here the water the cold water  which flows in there flows around that  inner tube it flows around it cooling  down the steam and as it does we have  condensation that's why it's called a  condenser because the steam as it's  cooled down turns back into liquid water  and there we have it right there so we  have clean water right here the salt  remains here because we do not get to  that temperature but the water is  separated turns to steam then as it goes  into the condenser if I was to touch  that it would be cold we turn it back  into liquid water the reason why we  constantly have the water flow in is to  make sure the water stays cool at all  times if we just had the water sat there  it would get warmed up by the steam and  wouldn't work as well so that's simple  distillation the next thing we're going  to look it up is fractional distillation  now the difference between the two is  fractional distillation is used when we  have a mixture of liquids with similar  boiling points or kind of much closer so  let's just imagine we have liquid a  which has a boiling point of let's say  60 degree C let's imagine we have liquid  well we have liquid B which has a  boiling point of 80 degrees C and we  have liquid see liquid C which has a  boiling point of 100 degree C 100  degrees C cool so at the beginning we've  got them all in here so we've got a  mixture of liquids here now we can't use  simple distillation because these  boiling points are all far too close to  each other they become out at the same  time if we use simple distillation or  rather similar times would be very  difficult so instead we have this  fractional distillation column and  towards the bottom of the column it's  hotter  to the heat and towards the top of the  column it's colder now we could tell the  temperature at the top of the column  because of this thermometer which is in  the top so what will happen is stage by  stage each liquid will come out just the  same as before this is the condenser and  the condenser works in the same way by  condensing the the the gas and turning  it back into a liquid so the first  liquid that's going to evaporate is  going to be liquid a it's going to  travel up the column and travel down  here when it gets the condenser that  will turn it back into liquid water if  let's say liquid B evaporates it might  get up to around here on the column and  then it might just drip back down  because it will condense here instead so  the first liquid you would separate  would be liquid a so you can collect  that whole liquid there first what you  could then do is let's say when this  thermometer then reaches 80 you could  then go get another conical flask and  collect your liquid B so you move that  conical flask out of the way because  hopefully all of your liquid a would  have evaporated and now you could  collect your liquid B and finally once  you've done that when this reaches a  hundred hopefully we should be  evaporating liquid C so you then move  that conical flask out of the way and  you can collect your liquid C so quick  recap fraction distillation is used to  separate when we have liquids with kind  of similar boiling points  unlike in simple distillation which we  use when the two different substances  have very different boiling points thank  you very much
 

